From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 18:12:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: several MoE exchanges in flight per expert
 node over MCDMA (TF_GLM_MCDMA_INFLIGHT), glm53f-afd's queued exchanges on the
 AFD split

Ash Hart (@ashxhart, github.com/ashhart) authored MCDMA (github.com/ashhart/MCDMA @ e672c14, Apache-2.0), whose link
daemons carry the attention/expert exchange: its protocol 1 has one mailbox and one request per link and no queue
mode, so exchanges in flight take more links. Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden) authored the exchanges
kept in flight ahead of the expert ranks in glm53f-afd (@ 91db3cc, crates/glm53f-serve/src/lib.rs lines 74-75 and
153-165: a prefill pass of N lanes keeps N exchanges in flight over RDMA, as many as the ranks queue, four by default
with glm53f-rank serve --recv-slots), MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Turquoise Bay AI Pty Ltd. Written here for MCDMA
protocol 1 and this tree's prompt chunk lanes; no code is copied.

TF_GLM_MCDMA_INFLIGHT=N (1 .. 4, the attention node only; unset or 1: one link to each expert node, every call,
header, buffer and byte as before):
- mcdma.py: inflight() reads the switch; link_names(N) gives each expert node N links (its TF_AFD_MCDMA_LINKS name,
  then NAME-1 .. NAME-(N-1)) and socket_for puts link j's listen socket beside link 0's; AttentionLinks.issue and
  collect take one exchange slot's links (one to each expert node); ExpertLink.stage_gpu takes a stream.
- afd_mcdma.py: McdmaTransport keeps N link pairs (its depth): the run's k-th request goes on pair k mod N and replies
  are collected in request order; each link's first sequence under its own store key; the handshake carries
  "inflight": N, which an expert node of an older tree refuses. The expert node opens its N links (each its own base
  and listen socket) and McdmaExpertLink takes the run's k-th request on link k mod N, landing and computing the
  requests in that order on its one compute stream, so the kernels and bits are those of one link. GPU-driven on
  CUDA, each link has its own routed rows (out): the reply is copied out and staged on a second stream after the
  compute's event, and the compute waits for a link's last copy before writing its rows again.
- experts_node.py: the compute writes into the link's rows when it has them (ExpertNode.compute's out); the expert
  echoes "inflight" in its settings.
- afd.py: AfdWire.depth is the transport's; a pair's or lanes' requests on the wire are a queue of at most depth
  (window, layer) entries, replies in request order; windows that close with requests unanswered are refused, after
  today's check of windows that stopped early (at depth 1 too: such a request would put the experts out of step).
- prefill_pairs.py: the lanes run on a wire of depth N > 1 (refused before): a lane's exchange goes out as soon as its
  attention ran while up to N - 1 earlier ones are unanswered, so the expert nodes find the next request waiting when
  they reply. Every lane then holds its own shared expert output (FLIGHT_FIELDS: sy, which moe_send writes before the
  lane's collect reads it; slot_buffers / lane_buffers with flight, buffers_for on a deeper wire), and lanes that share
  it are refused. The reply rows stay shared at any depth: the scheduler collects only when no lane can run, and a
  lane past its collect always can, so its hc_post reads them before the next collect.
- engine.py: the later lanes' own shared expert output at startup and in the capacity plan (32 MiB a lane at 2,048
  rows on GLM-5.3-Flash), named in the startup line.

Each extra link needs its own mcdma-rpcd listen daemon on the expert node and a peer of the attention node's connect
daemon (at most six per daemon); MCDMA itself is unchanged.

Tests: tests/test_glm_mcdma_inflight.py, every case at N = 1, 2 and 4: the setting, link map, sockets and handshake
(an older expert refuses); the lane schedule on four layer layouts and 2-4 lanes (lane order, replies in request
order, at most N in flight and N reached, each reply used before the next collect, at N >= 2 each lane's exchange
before the lane before it collects that layer); each lane's recorded kernel calls equal its solo chunk's over the real
forward, with held states; the real AfdWire, McdmaTransport and both experts' loops over N link pairs of the fake
MCDMA pair, CPU-, GPU-driven and mixed: lanes of 4, 3 and 2 through the real scheduler, plain forwards and a layer
slice give every reply of the serial run, the experts the same requests in the same order at every N, the requests
round-robin over the links; three MoE layers move the next forward to another link; lanes sharing the shared expert
output are right at N = 1 and wrong at N = 2; with lane 0's replies held, lane 1's request goes out and is computed
at N = 2 and waits at N = 1; windows that end with a request unanswered are refused at the next header (windows that
stopped early: today's message), and lanes refuse a wire of depth 0; the switch off issues and collects every link at
once; the expert's reply stream (faked streams and events); serve() over the store handshake with two links each.
tests/test_glm_prefill_lanes.py: a deeper wire now needs each lane's own sy.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

