+"""TF_GLM_MCDMA_INFLIGHT (several MoE exchanges in flight per expert node over N MCDMA link pairs) on CPU.
+
+MCDMA is Ash Hart's (@ashxhart, github.com/ashhart/MCDMA); keeping exchanges queued ahead of the expert ranks follows
+glm53f-afd by Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden/glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc). Every test runs N = 1 (the
+switch off: today's one link pair), 2 (the pairs') and 4 (TF_GLM_PREFILL_LANES=4's).
+
+- The setting, the link map (link j of a node is ``NAME-j``, its listen socket beside link 0's) and the handshake
+  (``inflight`` only when N > 1, echoed by the experts; an expert without it refuses).
+- The schedule (``prefill_pairs._forward_lanes`` over stand-in layer loops, 2-4 lanes): exchanges in lane order and
+  collected in that order, never more than N on the wire, a lane's reply used before any other collect (the reply
+  rows stay shared), and at N >= 2 each lane's exchange goes out before the lane before it collects that layer's reply.
+- The forward seam (tests/test_glm_prefill_pairs.py's recorded kernels over the real forward): at N = 1, 2 and 4 every
+  lane's kernel calls are its solo chunk's, its own shared expert output (``FLIGHT_FIELDS``) named as the solo's.
+- The wire: the real ``AfdWire`` + ``McdmaTransport`` and both experts' ``forwards`` over N link pairs of the fake
+  mcdma-rpcd pair, CPU-, GPU-driven and mixed: lanes of 4, 3 and 2 through the real scheduler, plain forwards and a
+  layer slice; every reply equals the serial run's, the experts saw the same requests in the same order at every N,
+  the requests went round-robin over the links; a forward of 3 MoE layers moves the next forward to another link.
+- B before A's reply: lane 0's replies held in the daemon; at N = 2 lane 1's request goes out and is computed while
+  lane 0's reply is held, at N = 1 it does not.
+- The switch off: N = 1 issues and collects every link at once (today's calls), publishes no ``inflight``.
+- The expert's reply stream (GPU-driven, faked streams and events): each link's own routed rows, the copy and the staged
+  word on the second stream after the compute's event, and the compute waits for a link's last copy before reusing
+  its rows.
+- serve(): the handshake with ``inflight`` 2, each expert opening its two links (bases per link) and serving.
+"""
+
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import contextlib
+import threading
+import time
+from types import SimpleNamespace
+
+import pytest
+
+from tests import test_glm_prefill_lanes as tl
